<article class="env-remark" id="0FGE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FGE">Remark <span data-tag="0FGE">45.4.5</span> <span class="named">(Lefschetz and Tate motive)</span>.</a>  Let $X = \mathbf{P}^1_ k$ and $c_2$ be as in Example <a href="/tag/0FG6" data-tag="0FG6">45.3.7</a>. In the literature the motive $(X, c_2, 0)$ is sometimes called the <em>Lefschetz motive</em> and depending on the reference the notation $L$, $\mathbf{L}$, $\mathbf{Q}(-1)$, or $h^2(\mathbf{P}^1_ k)$ may be used to denote it. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0FGD" data-tag="0FGD">45.4.4</a> the Lefschetz motive is isomorphic to $\mathbf{1}(-1)$. Hence the Lefschetz motive is invertible (Categories, Definition <a href="/tag/0FFN" data-tag="0FFN">4.43.7</a>) with inverse $\mathbf{1}(1)$. The motive $\mathbf{1}(1)$ is sometimes called the <em>Tate motive</em> and depending on the reference the notation $L^{-1}$, $\mathbf{L}^{-1}$, $\mathbf{T}$, or $\mathbf{Q}(1)$ may be used to denote it. </p>
